      <section class="article-section" id="unpaid-weeks">
        <h2>What Unpaid Weeks Cost</h2>
        <p>The $131,040.00 headline assumes 52 paid weeks. Unpaid time off, whether that is vacation without PTO, a seasonal gap, or a slow January, comes straight off the year. The take-home cost is bigger than the gross cost looks, because the weeks you lose are full-rate weeks:</p>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="pay-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Paid weeks</th><th>Hours paid</th><th>Annual gross</th><th>Federal-only take-home</th><th>Take-home lost</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td><strong>52 (no unpaid time)</strong></td><td>2,080</td><td><strong>$131,040.00</strong></td><td><strong>$100,831.84</strong></td><td>—</td></tr>
              <tr><td>51 (1 unpaid week)</td><td>2,040</td><td>$128,520.00</td><td>$99,109.42</td><td>$1,722.42</td></tr>
              <tr><td>50 (2 unpaid weeks)</td><td>2,000</td><td>$126,000.00</td><td>$97,387.00</td><td>$3,444.84</td></tr>
              <tr><td>48 (4 unpaid weeks)</td><td>1,920</td><td>$120,960.00</td><td>$93,925.36</td><td>$6,906.48</td></tr>
              <tr><td>39 (13 unpaid weeks)</td><td>1,560</td><td>$98,280.00</td><td>$77,969.98</td><td>$22,861.86</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>One unpaid week costs $2,520.00 gross and $1,722.42 of take-home. That is more than a $1-an-hour raise is worth ($1,421.68), which is the honest way to weigh a job with unpaid gaps against one with paid time off. And the rung drop that started at $56 is now a full step wider: one unpaid week at $63 ($99,109.42) falls $300.74 short of a full-time $62-an-hour year, while at $62 the same week fell only $273.40 short of a full-time $61-an-hour year. Two unpaid weeks cost $5,040.00 gross and $3,444.84 of take-home, yet still land $2,241.88 ahead of a full-time $59-an-hour year, though they fall $601.48 short of a full-time $61-an-hour year. Four unpaid weeks cost $10,080.00 gross and $6,906.48 of take-home, landing ahead of a full-time $58-an-hour year by $225.12, yet trailing a full-time $59-an-hour year by $1,219.76, though the year still beats a $100,000 salary by $20,960.00 gross and $14,745.36 of take-home. Thirteen unpaid weeks are the real flip on this page: $77,969.98 of take-home trails a $100,000 salary by $1,210.02. At $62, thirteen unpaid weeks trailed it by $2,307.48, so the gap is narrowing, but a missed quarter of the year still costs you the six-figure comparison. The near-misses are worth a look as well: one unpaid week at $63 still beats a full-time $60-an-hour year by $2,542.62, so the first unpaid week only drops you one rung, and thirteen unpaid weeks land on exactly the same $98,280.00 gross as a 30-hour week worked all year, which is why the schedules table and this table agree to the cent. Our <a href="/articles/how-much-is-62-an-hour-a-year/">$62-an-hour guide</a> and <a href="/articles/how-much-is-61-an-hour-a-year/">$61-an-hour guide</a> price the same unpaid-week steps at their rates, so the three guides read as one ladder.</p>
      </section>

